          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Deck paint vs deck stain — which should I use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Use stain on new or well-maintained wood where the grain should show. Use paint on weathered, cracked, or previously painted decks where the wood appearance is no longer the goal. Paint provides better coverage and crack-hiding; stain preserves natural wood beauty. Once you paint a deck, returning to stain requires complete stripping.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does deck paint last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Premium acrylic deck paints last 3–5 years in temperate climates. Marine enamels can last even longer. Standard porch paints typically last 2–3 years between recoats. UV exposure, foot traffic, surface prep, and climate all influence longevity significantly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over old deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, with proper preparation. Clean thoroughly, scuff-sand glossy areas, fill cracks with exterior filler, and prime if the stain is dark or you're using light paint. Resurfacers (DeckOver, Restore 10X) often self-prime over solid stain but still require thorough cleaning.</p>
