        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Deck Painting Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck paint is asked to do something no other exterior coating is: form a continuous film on horizontal wood that is walked on, rained on, and left to bake. The failures are predictable.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting a Deck That Should Be Stained</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint and thick resurfacers form a film, and a film on a horizontal surface will eventually trap moisture from below and peel. On sound, attractive wood a penetrating stain is the lower-maintenance choice. Paint earns its place on weathered, mismatched, or previously painted boards where hiding is the point.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Primer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bare or heavily weathered wood pulls the binder out of the first coat, and knots and tannin bleed through anything applied directly. A dedicated exterior wood primer is what gives the topcoat a uniform surface to grip. Painting straight onto bare boards is the fastest route to peeling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Underside and Edges</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water gets into a deck board through its ends and its underside far more than its face. Coating only what you can see leaves those routes open, and the moisture that enters pushes the film off from behind. Coat the board ends, and coat the undersides of any boards you can reach before laying them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying in Direct Sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A deck board in afternoon sun can sit 40°F above air temperature, which flashes the surface of the paint before it has flowed out or bonded. The result is lap marks, poor adhesion, and early failure. Follow the shade around the deck and stop while boards are still cool to the touch.
              </p>
            </div>
